NOOB.READ
Unified Vulnerability Aggregation & Prioritization Platform
A plain-English walkthrough for someone new to Python, networking, and cybersecurity


================================================================
0. READ THIS FIRST
================================================================

This project is a small software system that answers one practical question:

  "What is broken or insecure on my computers and network, and what should I
   fix first?"

Security teams use scanners to hunt for weaknesses (called vulnerabilities).
The problem is that you often have more than one scanner, they all speak
different "languages", and they dump hundreds of findings on you.

This platform:

  1. Asks two scanners (Greenbone/OpenVAS and Nessus) to scan the same targets
  2. Translates their different reports into one common format
  3. Looks up extra intel (is there a public exploit? how likely is it to be
     used in the wild?)
  4. Gives each finding a risk score from 0 to 10
  5. Saves everything in a database
  6. Lets you browse results in a web dashboard and a REST API

You do not need to be a security expert to understand the idea. Think of it
like two different doctors examining the same patient, a specialist adding
notes, and then a nurse ranking which symptoms to treat first.


================================================================
1. THE REAL-WORLD PROBLEM (WHY THIS EXISTS)
================================================================

Imagine you own a house.

  - One inspector checks the doors and windows (scanner A).
  - Another inspector checks the electrical wiring (scanner B).
  - A public database lists which house problems burglars actually exploit
    (threat intel).

If you just pile both inspector reports on the kitchen table, you get:

  - Duplicate notes ("front door lock is weak" appears twice)
  - Different wording for the same issue
  - No ranking of "fix this tonight" vs "fix this next month"

This project is the person who:

  - Sends both inspectors out at the same time
  - Rewrites every note onto the same form
  - Cross-checks a public "burglar playbook"
  - Circles the most urgent items
  - Files the forms in a cabinet you can search later


================================================================
2. CYBERSECURITY WORDS YOU WILL SEE EVERYWHERE
================================================================

These words show up in the code. Learn them once and the rest of the project
gets much easier.

VULNERABILITY
  A weakness that an attacker could abuse.
  Example: an old version of software with a known bug, a service with a
  default password, an unpatched Windows machine.

CVE (Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures)
  A public ID for a known vulnerability, like CVE-2021-44228.
  Think of it as a catalog number. If two scanners both say "CVE-2021-44228",
  they are talking about the same published weakness.

SCANNER
  A program that looks at computers / IP addresses and reports weaknesses.
  This project talks to two:
    - GVM / OpenVAS / Greenbone  (open-source scanner)
    - Nessus                     (commercial scanner from Tenable)

TARGET
  What you want scanned. It can be:
    - One computer:        192.168.1.10
    - A whole neighborhood of IPs:  192.168.1.0/24
    - A website name:      example.com
  The list lives in the .env file as SCAN_TARGETS.

ASSET
  The thing that has the vulnerability: a host name, an IP address, maybe
  an operating system. In this code you will see asset_host and asset_ip.

PORT
  A numbered "door" on a computer. Websites often use port 80 or 443.
  SSH (remote login) often uses port 22. A vulnerability is often tied to
  "this IP, this port, this service".

PROTOCOL
  The language used on that port, usually tcp or udp. You can think of TCP
  as a phone call that stays connected, and UDP as sending postcards.

SEVERITY
  A simple label: Low, Medium, High, Critical.
  Scanners assign this. It is a first guess, not the final ranking this
  project uses.

CVSS (Common Vulnerability Scoring System)
  A number from 0 to 10 that describes how bad a CVE is in theory.
  There are versions: CVSS v2 (older) and CVSS v3 (newer, preferred here).
  Example: 9.8 is almost as bad as it gets.

EPSS (Exploit Prediction Scoring System)
  A number from 0.0 to 1.0 that estimates the chance this CVE will be
  exploited in the wild soon. High CVSS + high EPSS is extra scary:
  "this is bad AND attackers actually use it".

EXPLOIT
  Working attack code / a known method to abuse the vulnerability.
  If exploit_available is True, someone has published a way to use it.
  That raises urgency even if the CVSS is only medium.

RISK SCORE (this project's own number)
  A 0-10 score this app computes. It mixes CVSS, EPSS, exploit availability,
  and (optionally) how important the machine is.
  The dashboard sorts by this so you fix the worst things first.

FINDING
  One row of "we found this weakness on this machine".
  One scan of a network can produce hundreds of findings.

THREAT INTELLIGENCE
  Extra facts from outside your network. This project uses three feeds:
  CISA KEV (actively exploited CVEs), FIRST EPSS (exploitation probability),
  and NVD/Vulners via ExploitIntelClient (CVSS v3.1 vectors and exploit signals).


================================================================
3. NETWORKING IN 2 MINUTES (ENOUGH TO READ THIS CODE)
================================================================

Computers on a network have addresses, like houses have street numbers.

IP ADDRESS
  Example: 192.168.1.15
  That is a private address, typically used inside a home or office LAN
  (Local Area Network). It is not the public address of the whole internet.

CIDR / SUBNET  (the "/24" you see)
  192.168.1.0/24 means "all addresses from 192.168.1.0 to 192.168.1.255".
  Scanning a subnet means "check this whole office floor", not one PC.

HOSTNAME
  A human name, like fileserver.office.local or example.com.
  DNS turns that name into an IP address.

SCANNING
  The scanner connects to the target, looks at open ports and software
  versions, and compares them to a database of known problems.
  This is normal defensive work on systems you own or have permission to
  test. Scanning other people's networks without permission is illegal.

API (Application Programming Interface)
  A way for programs to talk to each other over HTTP, like a website but
  for machines. You send a request, you get data back (usually JSON).

  This project:
    - CALLS APIs of GVM, Nessus, CISA KEV, FIRST EPSS, and NVD/Vulners
    - PROVIDES its own API so the dashboard (or you) can start scans and
      read findings

REST API
  A common style of HTTP API. You will see:
    POST /api/v1/scans              start a scan (async)
    GET  /api/v1/vulnerabilities    list findings
    GET  /api/v1/vulnerabilities/{id}  one finding
    GET  /api/v1/metrics/posture    security posture summary
    POST /api/v1/auth/token         log in and get a JWT
  GET means "read". POST means "do something / send data".

JSON
  A text format that looks like:
    { "cve_id": "CVE-2021-44228", "severity": "Critical" }
  Computers love it. Humans can still read it.


================================================================
4. PYTHON IDEAS USED IN THIS PROJECT (BEGINNER LEVEL)
================================================================

You do not need to be fluent in Python. These are the pieces this repo uses.

FILE = MODULE
  config.py is a Python file. Other files can "import" things from it.
  Example: from src.config import settings

FOLDER WITH __init__.py = PACKAGE
  src/, src/connectors/, src/api/, src/utils/ are packages.
  The empty __init__.py files tell Python "this folder is importable code,
  not just a pile of documents".

CLASS
  A blueprint. GVMConnector is a class. When the program does
  GVMConnector(), it creates one object that knows how to talk to GVM.

ASYNC / AWAIT / asyncio
  Normal Python does one thing, then the next.
  Scanning is mostly waiting: "I asked Nessus to scan, now I wait".
  Async lets Python start several waits at once (GVM and Nessus together)
  instead of sitting idle. You will see async def and await everywhere.
  You do not need to master it to follow the story.

ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES / .env
  Secrets and settings (passwords, API keys, host names) should not be
  hard-coded in the Python files. They live in a file named .env.
  src/config.py reads that file when the app starts.

DOCKER
  A way to run the app in isolated boxes called containers, so you do not
  have to install PostgreSQL, Redis, Python packages, etc. by hand on
  Windows. docker-compose.yml describes several boxes that start together.

POSTGRESQL
  A database. Think of a spreadsheet that programs can query.
  Findings are stored in a table named "vulnerabilities".

UPSERT
  "Update if this row already exists, insert if it is new."
  Recurring scans should refresh the same finding, not create a duplicate
  row every 6 hours.


================================================================
5. THE BIG PICTURE: HOW A SCAN FLOWS
================================================================

Here is the full journey of one scan, in order.

STEP A. Someone (or the clock) says "scan these targets"
  Sources:
    - You click "Trigger New Scan" on the dashboard
    - You POST to /api/v1/scans on the API
    - Celery Beat wakes up every SCAN_SCHEDULE_HOURS hours (default 6)

STEP B. ScanService takes over  (src/services/scan_service.py)
  This is the project manager. A Celery worker runs ScanService.execute().
  For every (scanner, target) pair, it calls the connector's three-step
  lifecycle: start_scan -> poll -> fetch_and_normalize.
  asyncio.gather waits until all finish (or fail). If one scanner crashes,
  the other scanner's results are still kept.

STEP C. Each connector talks to its scanner
  GVM:
    login -> create a target -> create a task -> start task -> poll until
    done (max 2 hours) -> download XML report -> turn XML into Vulnerability
    objects

  Nessus:
    create a scan -> launch it -> poll until completed (max 1 hour) ->
    fetch details -> turn JSON into Vulnerability objects

  Both connectors output the SAME Python object type: NormalizedVulnerability.
  That translation is called "normalization". Without it, you would have
  two incompatible report formats forever.

STEP D. Enrichment via threat-intel pipeline  (src/enrichment/pipeline.py)
  For every finding that has a CVE ID, EnrichmentPipeline queries:
    - CISA KEV: is this CVE actively exploited in the wild?
    - FIRST EPSS: exploitation probability and percentile
    - NVD/Vulners (ExploitIntelClient): CVSS v3.1 vector and exploit signals
  Findings without a CVE skip this step.

STEP E. Risk score  (src/engine/risk_engine.py)
  Each finding gets a number 0-10:
    - 40% from CVSS (prefer v3, else v2, else severity fallback)
    - 30% from EPSS (stretched to 0-10 scale)
    - 15% if a weaponized public exploit exists
    - KEV override: +1.5 boost and floor of 9.0 if on CISA KEV list
    - then multiplied by asset_criticality (0.5x to 1.5x; default 1.0)
  The score is capped at 10.

STEP F. Deduplicate this batch
  If the same scanner, plugin/vuln id, IP, and port appear twice in one
  run, keep the copy with the higher risk score.

STEP G. Save to PostgreSQL
  Each finding gets a stable ID (a short hash of scanner + vuln id + IP +
  port). If that ID already exists from a previous scan, the row is UPDATED
  (new score, new timestamp, new exploit info). If it is new, it is INSERTED.
  That is why rerunning scans does not flood the table with clones.

STEP H. You look at the results
  - Dashboard: Streamlit UI (dashboard/app.py) — log in, then view findings
  - API docs:  http://localhost:8000/docs  (interactive "try it" page)
  - GET /api/v1/vulnerabilities returns the worst findings first (highest risk_score)


